# id: BM115-block-needed-unreported
# blinds: a short block reporting the capacities the pair NEEDS
# covering-lane: cpp-test
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# The patched boundary still refuses correctly, still consumes nothing, and
# still holds the pair. Every property the previous test asserted is intact.
# What it stops reporting is HOW MUCH TO GROW BY -- it hands back the bytes it
# used, which on this path is always zero.
#
# THIS IS A MUTANT WHOSE DAMAGE IS ENTIRELY IN THE CALLER. Nothing in the engine
# reads these fields; the C++ suite passed for the whole of B5.0-B5.1 with the
# boundary reporting zero here, because no C++ caller ever grew. The defect
# surfaces one language away, as a caller that either doubles blindly or gives
# up on a value it could have read.
#
# It is caught only because CApi.ABlockTooSmallForOnePairIsToldTheCapacitiesItNeeds
# asserts the two numbers rather than only the status code -- which is the whole
# reason that test checks 7 and 5000 by name instead of checking that the call
# refused.
--- a/engine-cpp/capi/rift.cc
+++ b/engine-cpp/capi/rift.cc
@@ -336,8 +336,8 @@
           // "too small" has to GUESS how much to grow, and a guess that is
           // still too small loops. Reporting the requirement makes the retry
           // exact and makes the caller's loop terminate in one step.
-          if (keys_used != nullptr) *keys_used = k.size();
-          if (vals_used != nullptr) *vals_used = v.size();
+          if (keys_used != nullptr) *keys_used = ko;
+          if (vals_used != nullptr) *vals_used = vo;
           return RIFT_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL;
         }
         if (keys_used != nullptr) *keys_used = ko;
